WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.410
RETO MEIER: We couldn't
get enough of him.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.370
So we brought him back.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.570
Joining us once more is Kelsey
Hightower, developer advocate

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.100
at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.680
Kelsey, talk to us about
how serverless solutions

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.525
help developers.

00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:18.900
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yes, so I think

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.268
serverless solutions for
people new to serverless,

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:23.310
it's kind of like we've
learned a lot of patterns

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.227
from four or five
years of Kubernetes.

00:00:25.227 --> 00:00:27.810
And all the things you need to
do to provide that higher level

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.602
interface that you give
all your developers

00:00:29.602 --> 00:00:31.920
or that you enjoy working
with every day, that's where

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.660
serverless attempts to start.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.190
So we take some
of those patterns

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.050
and we give people the smallest
interface that they want to do.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.830
So if you think about if a file
gets uploaded to a Google Cloud

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.830
Storage bucket, you can just
invoke your business logic

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.417
and execute your code for you.

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.250
STEPHANIE WONG: Now,
people want serverless,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.910
but they also want containers.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.670
How is Google
enabling developers

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.350
to have both the
velocity of serverless

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.210
and the flexibility
of containers?

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:54.610
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
That's the thing

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:55.860
that I'm probably
the most proud of.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.270
So when serverless is most
introduced for first introduced

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.103
to most people, they
always thought about it

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:01.458
as functions as a service.

00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:03.000
You write a single
function, an event

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.687
comes in, it calls
your function.

00:01:04.687 --> 00:01:06.270
But there's a lot
of existing business

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.687
logic that needs
a little bit more

00:01:07.687 --> 00:01:08.857
than just a single function.

00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:10.440
Maybe there's some
bootstrapping code.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.960
You got a set of
things like logging,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.335
but a little bit
more flexibility

00:01:13.335 --> 00:01:14.580
and your choice of runtime.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.913
So what we decided to do was
take a lot of the learnings

00:01:16.913 --> 00:01:19.500
from Kubernetes and allow
developers to deploy

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.890
their own Docker container.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.640
So any runtime that you want
to use, any business logic

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.410
that you want, any
libraries, and we're

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.630
going to execute that container
just like we do with our Cloud

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.130
Function.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.630
So they scale to zero.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.463
They scale up quick.

00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:33.380
And they give you the
same serverless workflow

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.090
that you're used to without
abandoning all the good things

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.282
that come with containers.

00:01:37.282 --> 00:01:37.990
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.907
So you've talked a little
bit about serverless

00:01:39.907 --> 00:01:42.790
isn't brand new, but we're
changing the way that we're

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:43.940
sort of approaching it.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.315
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:01:45.315 --> 00:01:48.260
about how Google is changing
that serverless experience?

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.802
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
So I think, for us,

00:01:49.802 --> 00:01:51.870
is that our DNA has always
been around this idea

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.630
of this kind of software
delivery chain, right?

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.880
So this idea that when
you build a software,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.860
we like to build it once,
we sign those images,

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.170
and then we decide where we
want them to be deployed.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.880
So that means giving
people a bit of choice.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.255
So typically,
serverless has been

00:02:05.255 --> 00:02:07.882
kind of these proprietary
cloud platforms.

00:02:07.882 --> 00:02:09.840
Once you're in there,
you're kind of locked in.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.930
What we decided to do is make
sure that it runs everywhere.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.430
So wherever you as
a developer want

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.550
to run, just like most
Google tools and libraries,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.410
we allow our serverless stack
to run wherever you are.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.510
So I think what we're
doing different here

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.010
is taking some of the
things that worked well

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.907
in the Kubernetes space
and combining them

00:02:24.907 --> 00:02:26.490
with some of the
developer experiences

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.130
that we know that
people are looking for.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.255
And it's combining the two.

00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:32.310
So flexibility without losing
the tradeoffs and benefits

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:33.210
of going serverless.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.418
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:02:34.418 --> 00:02:37.320
So how does our serverless
offering help our developers,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.770
and how is it different?

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.270
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I think where it's

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.660
different is that you get to
use a familiar tool chain that

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.077
has been popularized over the
last couple of years, right?

00:02:45.077 --> 00:02:46.890
So containers are
the way most people

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.598
think about having full control
over their own software.

00:02:50.598 --> 00:02:52.140
Then to be able to
take that software

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.390
and outsource all of
the delivery, right?

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.510
So security, IEM
integration, being

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.060
able to connect to things
like Spanner and BigQuery,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.580
taking all of that kind
of toil away from you,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.950
and just say, hey,
checking your containers,

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.460
and we'll run them for you.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.380
So what we're
trying to do is kind

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.140
of make it very seamless
from a developer.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.170
Once you go from
idea to deployed,

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.920
we're just trying to give
you that same GCP experience

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.090
at the container level.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:15.798
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:03:15.798 --> 00:03:17.310
Now how do our
serverless offerings

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.955
give developers a more
open and portable offering

00:03:19.955 --> 00:03:21.330
so that customers
can potentially

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.497
move their workloads around?

00:03:22.497 --> 00:03:24.247
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So
one thing-- the trend

00:03:24.247 --> 00:03:26.880
that has been really prevalent
in GCP is in Kubernetes,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.068
we started with Docker, popular
open source container runtime,

00:03:30.068 --> 00:03:31.860
and then we shipped an
open source project,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.985
Kubernetes, which is the
underlying platform for GKE.

00:03:34.985 --> 00:03:37.110
And then when we thought
about entering serverless,

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.130
so we've had App Engine for
it for almost 11 years now.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.570
And we decided that let's--
what would we do if we wanted

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.460
to make App Engine
to be more open?

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.830
So we decided to start
with the Knative project.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.815
So Knative has a lot of
serverless components.

00:03:48.815 --> 00:03:50.940
You can think of it as a
serverless toolkit, things

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.220
like bills, a
function framework.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.387
And most of that stuff is
laid on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:55.387 --> 00:03:57.137
So what we've done is
we've taken the best

00:03:57.137 --> 00:03:59.370
parts of Kubernetes--
again, those design patterns

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.330
that we talked about.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.550
How do I just run
my application?

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.460
We've extracted that out into
a high level set of libraries

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:06.660
called Knative.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.020
And there's a hosted
version we call Cloud Run.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.642
And we also give you the ability
to run Cloud Run on top of GKE.

00:04:12.642 --> 00:04:14.100
So the benefit
there is that if you

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.340
need to do some
machine learning,

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.980
you can take that
serverless workload

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.180
and run it on a specialized
VM with a GPU or TPU.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.850
So you're no longer restricted
to just our serverless

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.020
runtime environments.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.020
You now have the whole of
GCP opened up to you, even

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.160
in the serverless context.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.460
STEPHANIE WONG: And you
touched on this a bit.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.890
But a lot of our
customers tell us

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.450
that they have
custom requirements,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.880
like specific run
times, custom binaries,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.380
or workload portability.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.820
And more often
than not, they turn

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.240
to containers for an answer.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.300
Can you talk about
how RGKE serverless

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.980
add-on allows you to bring
serverless to containers?

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:45.120
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yes.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.200
So when you think about--

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.150
a lot of these abstractions
change over time.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.250
We went from mainframe,
to physical machines,

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.010
to virtualization, to
higher level platforms,

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.840
like App Engine.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.210
What we now have with the
container image, this ability

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.930
to build a code that you want,
use the runtime that you want,

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.340
any version that you want--

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.260
and now that becomes
a portable bit.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:04.910
You can run this on
a virtual machine.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.180
You can run this on GKE.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.430
You can run this on Cloud Run.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.140
And you can also run
it on App Engine.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.557
So I think what
the developer gets

00:05:10.557 --> 00:05:12.380
is this ability to
truly write once

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.275
and deploy wherever they want.

00:05:14.275 --> 00:05:15.650
RETO MEIER: Now,
while we believe

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.920
Google Cloud is a great place
to run all types of workloads,

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.780
some customers,
we know that they

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.760
need to run on-premises or
across multiple clouds, right?

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.600
So tell us a little
bit more about Knative

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.130
and its role in
giving developers

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.830
the freedom to move stateless
containers pretty much

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:31.340
anywhere?

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.940
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah, so our
contribution to the ecosystem

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.887
started with maybe
Kubernetes, right?

00:05:36.887 --> 00:05:38.720
So Kubernetes, over the
four or five years--

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.637
you can see it now--
it's deployed and offered

00:05:40.637 --> 00:05:42.200
by every major cloud provider.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.640
Lots of people are already
running it on-prem.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.500
So it has wide adoption.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.070
So our strategy of
layering Knative

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.200
on top of Kubernetes,
this allows us

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.875
to kind of reap those benefits.

00:05:52.875 --> 00:05:54.500
So the tools that
you're familiar with,

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.150
we layer Knative on top.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.190
So that gives us that
portability framework

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:58.760
underneath.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.218
So, no matter where
you're running,

00:06:00.218 --> 00:06:03.350
whether it's on-prem, different
cloud provider, on GCP,

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.940
you can get the
same APIs, right?

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.850
So we talked earlier about
automating those APIs, that's

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.347
what Kubernetes represents.

00:06:09.347 --> 00:06:10.430
And I've always said this.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.650
Kubernetes is a platform
for building platforms.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.608
And we've chosen to build
our newest serverless

00:06:14.608 --> 00:06:16.310
platforms on top of Kubernetes.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.227
STEPHANIE WONG: Can you
talk a little bit more

00:06:18.227 --> 00:06:20.510
about how Knative provides
inventing and integrating

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:22.950
events from Google or
third party services

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.930
so you can really extend
your applications?

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.540
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So the big
thing to know about Knative,

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.280
it's not just Google's
project, right?

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.960
So now we have so
many contributors--

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.155
Red Hat, Cloud Foundry.

00:06:32.155 --> 00:06:33.530
So a lot of the
things that we've

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.290
seen in the eventing space,
like what is an event,

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.960
how should we accept it, a
lot of those open protocols,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.740
like Cloud Events, all of
that's supported inside Knative.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.680
So Knative gives you a
couple of components.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:44.990
We call Knative serving.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:45.800
Run my container.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:47.330
Scale it to zero.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.272
Start it again when
a request comes in.

00:06:49.272 --> 00:06:50.730
Then you have the
event team piece.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:51.990
So there's an event.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.140
And what we wanted to do
is make an event portable.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.432
So different cloud providers
has a different collection

00:06:56.432 --> 00:06:58.380
of events, different
transports for events.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.880
So we wanted to do is make sure
that the event framework was

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:01.628
very flexible.

00:07:01.628 --> 00:07:03.170
If you want to
integrate with GitHub,

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.720
or GCP, or some other
service that you have

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.127
that you've defined yourself,
that's also included.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.460
So what we've done is we've
given all of these framework

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.335
pieces, and what we've
done with Cloud Run is

00:07:12.335 --> 00:07:14.370
we've kind of curated the
perfect stack for you.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.120
RETO MEIER: Great.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.430
And is there a
particular way in which

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.840
we're able to solve that
problem that you describe,

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.700
as like having all
of these things

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.947
leading to all of
these different places?

00:07:24.947 --> 00:07:26.780
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yes,
so I think for us is,

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.520
Kubernetes is super flexible.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.830
So what that flexibility
allows every cloud provider

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.120
to do their own integrations.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.040
For example,
on-prem, we come out

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.220
of the gate integrating with
things like with VMware and F5.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.110
So we abstract away
some of the differences

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.710
between all of these platforms.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.700
So that's what
Kubernetes' job is.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.510
Once you have that
set of abstractions,

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.790
things like Knative
just sit on top

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:49.400
and naively assume that
those abstractions are there

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.890
and that we can get on with
building those higher level

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:52.790
platforms.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.900
So Kubernetes is a
huge part of this.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.650
And the community has
been doing this work

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.130
for over four years
of abstracting away

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.138
the differences between
these cloud providers.

00:08:00.138 --> 00:08:01.680
So whenever you
build on top of that,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.950
we just inherit all
of those benefits.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:04.910
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
thank you so much again

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.200
for joining us today, Kelsey.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:06.440
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:07.315
Thanks for having me.

00:08:07.315 --> 00:08:11.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

